## Setting up the function $f$

**Claim:** The conditions uniquely determine $f$, and $f(z)$ equals the sum of the continued fraction coefficients of $z$.

**Proof of uniqueness and characterization.** Every $z \in \mathbb{Q}_{\geq 0}$ can be reduced to $0$ via the Euclidean algorithm:
- If $z = 0$: $f(z) = 0$ (given).
- If $z \geq 1$: write $z = n + w$ with $n = \lfloor z \rfloor \geq 1$, $0 \leq w < 1$. Then $f(z) = f(w) + n$ by applying $f(z+1)=f(z)+1$ backwards $n$ times.
- If $0 < z < 1$: $f(z) = f(1/z)$ by condition 2, and $1/z > 1$, reducing to the previous case.

This process terminates (Euclidean algorithm on numerator/denominator). If $z = [a_0; a_1, \ldots, a_n]$ (continued fraction), the algorithm yields $f(z) = a_0 + a_1 + \cdots + a_n$.

